<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Charles VI of France</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1368">1368</a>–<a href="/years/1422">1422</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Charles the Mad · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-valois">House of Valois</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Charles VI, called the Beloved and later the Mad, was King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> from 1380 to 1422. His recurring bouts of insanity from 1392 left power to rival princes and led to civil war between the Armagnac and Burgundian factions. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-v-of-england">Henry V of England</a> exploited the conflict, winning at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-agincourt">Agincourt</a> in 1415, and by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-troyes">Treaty of Troyes</a> in 1420 Charles disinherited his son in favour of Henry.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-vi-of-france.jpg" alt="Charles VI of France" width="320" height="396" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Attributed to Mazarine Master · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Charles_VI_de_France_-_Dialogues_de_Pierre_Salmon_-_Bib_de_Gen%C3%A8ve_MsFr165f4.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Charles VI was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in the Hôtel Saint-Pol, on 3 December 1368, the son of Charles V and Joanna of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a>. His father died on 16 September 1380, leaving a favourable military situation, and Charles was crowned at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reims">Reims</a> on 4 November, aged eleven. His uncles ruled as regents: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-the-bold">Philip the Bold</a>, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>, Louis I, Duke of Anjou, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-duke-of-berry">John, Duke of Berry</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-ii-duke-of-bourbon">Louis II, Duke of Bourbon</a>. Philip took the dominant role.</p><p>During the regency the treasury built up by Charles V was squandered for the dukes' profit, and the reimposition of unpopular taxes led to revolts known as the Harelle. In 1388 Charles stripped his uncles of their positions and restored his father's advisers, the Marmousets.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In 1392, leading an expedition against the Duchy of Brittany, Charles suffered his first attack of delirium in the forest near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-mans">Le Mans</a>, and killed several of his own men. From then until his death he alternated between mental instability and lucidity. The cause of his illness is unknown; theories include schizophrenia, manic-depressive psychosis, porphyria and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/typhus">typhus</a>-induced encephalopathy. He believed at times that he was made of glass, and in one episode he claimed to be Saint George.</p><p>On 29 January 1393 the Bal des Ardents, a masked ball in which his costume was set alight by a torch, left four dancers dead. In September 1394 he ordered that no Jew should dwell in his domains.</p><p>Queen Isabeau and the grandees governed in his name. The enmity between his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-i-duke-of-orleans">Louis I, Duke of Orléans</a>, and Philip's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-the-fearless">John the Fearless</a> led to the murder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Orléans</a> in 1407 and to the Armagnac–Burgundian Civil War, which lasted until 1435. John the Fearless was himself killed on 10 September 1419 at Montereau, in a meeting with the Dauphin, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-the-good">Philip the Good</a> then sided with the English.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-v-of-england">Henry V of England</a> invaded and defeated the French army at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-agincourt">Agincourt</a> on 25 October 1415. By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-troyes">Treaty of Troyes</a> of 21 May 1420 Henry was named Charles's successor, the Dauphin was disinherited, and Charles's daughter Catherine of Valois was betrothed to Henry. Charles died on 21 October 1422 at the Hôtel Saint-Pol and was buried at Saint-Denis. Henry V had died a few weeks earlier, so the infant Henry VI of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> became king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> under the treaty. The disinherited Dauphin, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joan-of-arc">Joan of Arc</a>'s help, was crowned Charles VII at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reims">Reims</a> on 17 July 1429, and the war ended in 1453 with a Valois victory.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Charles VI of France” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>But during his visit to Paris in 1378 he appointed the French dauphin, the future <b>Charles VI</b>, imperial vicar of the kingdom for life, effectively surrendering imperial authority in the Rhône valley.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-arles">Kingdom of Arles · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Factional struggle between Guelph and Ghibelline clans and between nobles and popolari remained endemic, and Genoa repeatedly submitted to foreign lords: <b>Charles VI of France</b> in 1396–1409, the Visconti of Milan in 1421–1435 and the Sforza after 1464.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-genoa">Republic of Genoa · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Henry V of England rode into Paris alongside <b>Charles VI of France</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-1">1 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>King <b>Charles VI of France</b> granted the people of Roquefort-sur-Soulzon a monopoly on ripening Roquefort cheese, as they had done for centuries.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-4">4 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1404 his son Charles III of Navarre exchanged Évreux with <b>Charles VI</b> for the duchy of Nemours, and it returned to the Kingdom of France.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-evreux">County of Évreux</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1399 Count Archambaud VI was condemned for treason and his county confiscated; <b>Charles VI</b> gave it in 1400 to his brother Louis of Orléans.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-perigord">County of Périgord</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.86,2.35&amp;year=1403" aria-label="Paris on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Charles VI of France"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5132 5945h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5132 5945h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.86,2.35&amp;year=1403">What was here in 1403? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
